        <!-- Section: Formulas and Laws -->
        <section class="article-section article-section--alt" id="section-formulas">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 17c2 0 2.8-1 2.8-2.8V10c0-2 1-3.3 3.2-3"/><path d="M9 11.2h5.7"/></svg></span> Formulas</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Formula for Racine Carrée and Racine Cubique</h2>

                <h3 class="article-h3">Square Root (Racine Carrée) Formula</h3>
                <div class="math-block">
                    <span class="math-formula">√x = y &nbsp;⟺&nbsp; y² = x &nbsp;and&nbsp; y ≥ 0</span>
                </div>
                <p>This defines the <em>principal square root</em> — always the non-negative result.</p>

                <h3 class="article-h3">Cube Root (Racine Cubique) Formula</h3>
                <div class="math-block">
                    <span class="math-formula">∛x = y &nbsp;⟺&nbsp; y³ = x &nbsp;for all real x</span>
                </div>
                <p>Unlike the square root, this is valid for all real numbers — positive, negative, and zero.</p>

                <h3 class="article-h3">The Five Core Laws for Both Operations</h3>

                <div class="law-cards">
                    <div class="law-card">
                        <div class="law-header">
                            <div class="law-number">1</div>
                            <div>
                                <div class="law-label">Product Rule</div>
                                <div class="law-subtitle">Split products inside radicals</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">ⁿ√(a × b) = ⁿ√a × ⁿ√b</span></div>
                        <p class="law-example"><strong>Square root:</strong> √(4 × 9) = √4 × √9 = 2 × 3 = 6<br><strong>Cube root:</strong> ∛(8 × 27) = ∛8 × ∛27 = 2 × 3 = 6</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="law-card">
                        <div class="law-header">
                            <div class="law-number">2</div>
                            <div>
                                <div class="law-label">Quotient Rule</div>
                                <div class="law-subtitle">Split quotients inside radicals</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">ⁿ√(a/b) = ⁿ√a / ⁿ√b &nbsp;(b ≠ 0)</span></div>
                        <p class="law-example"><strong>Example:</strong> √(100/25) = √100 / √25 = 10/5 = 2</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="law-card">
                        <div class="law-header">
                            <div class="law-number">3</div>
                            <div>
                                <div class="law-label">Root Cancellation</div>
                                <div class="law-subtitle">Inverse of exponentiation</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">(√a)² = a &nbsp;and&nbsp; (∛a)³ = a</span></div>
                        <p class="law-example">This is the fundamental inverse relationship — raising a root to its index cancels the radical entirely.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="law-card">
                        <div class="law-header">
                            <div class="law-number">4</div>
                            <div>
                                <div class="law-label">Absolute Value</div>
                                <div class="law-subtitle">Square root only — always positive</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">√(a²) = |a|</span></div>
                        <p class="law-example"><strong>Critical:</strong> √((−5)²) = √25 = 5, not −5. The principal square root is always non-negative.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="law-card law-card--full">
                        <div class="law-header">
                            <div class="law-number">5</div>
                            <div>
                                <div class="law-label">Sign Preservation</div>
                                <div class="law-subtitle">Cube root only — preserves sign</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="math-block"><span class="math-formula">∛(a³) = a &nbsp;(no absolute value needed)</span></div>
                        <p class="law-example">∛((−7)³) = ∛(−343) = −7. Unlike square roots, the cube root preserves the sign of the input — no imaginary numbers needed.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="warning-box">
                    <div class="warning-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m21.73 18-8-14a2 2 0 0 0-3.48 0l-8 14A2 2 0 0 0 4 21h16a2 2 0 0 0 1.73-3Z"/><line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/></svg></div>
                    <div class="warning-content">
                        <strong>The Addition Trap — Most Common Algebra Error</strong>
                        <div class="math-block" style="margin-top: 12px;"><span class="math-formula">√(a + b) ≠ √a + √b &nbsp;(same for cube roots)</span></div>
                        <p>Always verify: √(9+16) = √25 = 5, not √9 + √16 = 3 + 4 = 7 ✗</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section: Step-by-Step Methods for Square Root -->
        <section class="article-section" id="section-methods-sqrt">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="4" y1="9" x2="20" y2="9"/><line x1="4" y1="15" x2="20" y2="15"/><line x1="10" y1="3" x2="8" y2="21"/><line x1="16" y1="3" x2="14" y2="21"/></svg></span> Methods</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">How to Calculate a Racine Carrée — Step-by-Step Methods</h2>

                <h3 class="article-h3">1. Estimation by Perfect Squares</h3>
                <p>The fastest mental method for <em>calculer la racine carrée</em> without any tools.</p>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Find √55</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>7² = 49 and 8² = 64, so 7 &lt; √55 &lt; 8</li>
                        <li>55 is closer to 49, so estimate ≈ 7.4</li>
                        <li>Test 7.42: 7.42² = 55.06 → ≈ 7.416 ✓</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="article-h3">2. Prime Factorization Method</h3>
                <p>Ideal for exact results and simplified radical form.</p>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Simplify √72</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>72 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 = 2² × 3² × 2</li>
                        <li>Extract pairs: √72 = 2 × 3 × √2 = 6√2 ≈ 8.485</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Simplify √180</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>180 = 2² × 3² × 5</li>
                        <li>√180 = 2 × 3 × √5 = 6√5 ≈ 13.416</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="article-h3">3. Long Division Method</h3>
                <p>The classic algorithm — accurate to any decimal place, no tools needed.</p>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Calculate √529</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>Group digits: 5 | 29</li>
                        <li>First group (5): largest d where d² ≤ 5 → d = 2 (2² = 4)</li>
                        <li>Remainder: 5 − 4 = 1; bring down 29 → 129</li>
                        <li>Trial divisor: 40; find q: 43 × 3 = 129 ✓</li>
                        <li>√529 = 23 ✓ — Verify: 23² = 529 ✓</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="article-h3">4. Newton-Raphson Method (What Powers Our Calculator)</h3>
                <div class="math-block">
                    <span class="math-formula">x<sub>n+1</sub> = ½ (x<sub>n</sub> + S / x<sub>n</sub>)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="table-scroll-wrapper">
                    <div class="table-scroll-hint">
                        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 8L22 12L18 16"/><path d="M6 8L2 12L6 16"/><line x1="2" y1="12" x2="22" y2="12"/></svg>
                        Scroll horizontally →
                    </div>
                    <table class="data-table data-table--compact">
                        <thead><tr><th>Iteration</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>x₀</td><td>1.500000</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>x₁</td><td>1.416667</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>x₂</td><td>1.414216</td></tr>
                            <tr class="table-highlight"><td>x₃</td><td>1.41421356 ✓</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section: Step-by-Step Methods for Cube Root -->
        <section class="article-section article-section--alt" id="section-methods-cbrt">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="4" y="4" width="16" height="16" rx="2" ry="2"/><line x1="8" y1="4" x2="8" y2="20"/><line x1="16" y1="4" x2="16" y2="20"/><line x1="4" y1="8" x2="8" y2="8"/><line x1="4" y1="16" x2="8" y2="16"/><line x1="16" y1="12" x2="20" y2="12"/></svg></span> Cube Root Methods</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">How to Calculate a Racine Cubique — Step-by-Step Methods</h2>

                <h3 class="article-h3">1. Estimation by Perfect Cubes</h3>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Find ∛90</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, so 4 &lt; ∛90 &lt; 5</li>
                        <li>90 is 43% of the way from 64 to 125 → estimate ≈ 4.43</li>
                        <li>Test 4.48: 4.48³ ≈ 89.9 ✓ → ∛90 ≈ 4.481</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="article-h3">2. Prime Factorization for Cube Roots</h3>
                <p>Instead of pairs, group factors into <strong>triples</strong>.</p>
                <div class="method-example">
                    <div class="method-title">Example: Simplify ∛54</div>
                    <ul class="method-steps-list">
                        <li>54 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 2 × 3³</li>
                        <li>One triple of 3s; the 2 is unpaired</li>
                        <li>∛54 = 3 × ∛2 = 3∛2 ≈ 3.780</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="article-h3">3. Newton-Raphson for Cube Roots</h3>
                <div class="math-block">
                    <span class="math-formula">x<sub>n+1</sub> = ⅓ (2x<sub>n</sub> + S / x<sub>n</sub>²)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="table-scroll-wrapper">
