        <!-- Section: FAQ -->
        <section class="article-section article-section--alt" id="faq-section">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M9.09 9a3 3 0 0 1 5.83 1c0 2-3 3-3 3"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/></svg></span> FAQ</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

                <div class="faq-list">
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-1">
                        <summary class="faq-question">What does "racine carrée" mean in English?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>"Racine carrée" is French for "square root." Our racine carree calculator is named to serve both French and English-speaking users worldwide. In French, "racine" means root and "carrée" means square.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-2">
                        <summary class="faq-question">How do I use the racine carree calculator?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Simply type any number into the input box at the top of this page. The calculator instantly displays the square root (racine carrée) or cube root (racine cubique), simplified radical form, and step-by-step workings. No button press needed — results appear in real time.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-3">
                        <summary class="faq-question">Can I calculer la racine carrée of a negative number?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. When you enter a negative number, the racine carree calculator returns an imaginary result using the unit i. For example, √(−25) = 5i. For cube roots, negative inputs return real negative results: ∛(−27) = −3.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-4">
                        <summary class="faq-question">What is the difference between racine carrée and racine cubique?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>The racine carrée (√) finds y where y² = x — defined only for non-negative numbers in the reals, always returns a positive result. The racine cubique (∛) finds y where y³ = x — defined for all real numbers, preserves the sign of the input. Our calculator handles both with a single mode switch.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-5">
                        <summary class="faq-question">How do I calculer une racine carrée manually?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Use the estimation method (bracket between perfect squares), prime factorization for exact forms, or the long division algorithm for any precision. All three methods are explained in full with worked examples above.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-6">
                        <summary class="faq-question">Is this racine carree calculator free?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes — completely. No subscription, no registration, no ads interrupting your workflow. Our calcul racine carrée tool is available 24/7 on any device, at no cost.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-7">
                        <summary class="faq-question">What is the racine carrée of 2?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>√2 is an irrational number — its decimal expansion never ends and never repeats. To 10 decimal places: 1.4142135624. It is approximately 1.41421356237...</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-8">
                        <summary class="faq-question">What is the racine cubique of 0?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>∛0 = 0. Zero is the only number whose cube root equals itself (0³ = 0). The same is true for the square root: √0 = 0.</p></div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq-item" id="faq-9">
                        <summary class="faq-question">How precise is this calculator?</summary>
                        <div class="faq-answer"><p>The racine carree calculator uses IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point arithmetic — the international standard for scientific software — giving up to 15 significant digits of accuracy. The displayed precision is up to 10 decimal places.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

