                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 16v-4"/><path d="M12 8h.01"/></svg></span> Pro Tips</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Pro Tips for Working With Racine Carrée and Racine Cubique</h2>

                <div class="tips-grid">
                    <div class="tip-card">
                        <div class="tip-number">1</div>
                        <p><strong>Memorize perfect squares to 20 and perfect cubes to 15.</strong> These two lists unlock fast mental estimation for both operations.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tip-card">
                        <div class="tip-number">2</div>
                        <p><strong>Use simplified radical form in algebra.</strong> The form 6√2 (for √72) or 3∛2 (for ∛54) is more precise than a rounded decimal and is the expected format in exams.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tip-card">
                        <div class="tip-number">3</div>
                        <p><strong>Remember: the addition trap applies to both.</strong> √(a+b) ≠ √a + √b, and ∛(a+b) ≠ ∛a + ∛b. Most common algebra error at every level.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tip-card">
                        <div class="tip-number">4</div>
                        <p><strong>For negative numbers, know which tool to use.</strong> √(−x) = i√x (imaginary). ∛(−x) = −∛x (real). Our calculator handles both automatically.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tip-card">
                        <div class="tip-number">5</div>
                        <p><strong>Always verify by reversing the operation.</strong> After finding √x ≈ y, check y² ≈ x. After finding ∛x ≈ y, check y³ ≈ x. Our calculator displays this automatically.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
